{{also|post-humous}}
==English==

===Alternative forms===
* {{l|en|post-humous}}
* {{alter|en|postumous||archaic}}

===Etymology===
From {{bor|en|la|posthumus}}, a variant spelling of {{m|la|postumus}}, superlative form of {{m|la|posterus|t=coming after}}, the ⟨h⟩ added by association with {{m|la|humus|t=ground, earth}} referring to burial.

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|UK|US}} {{IPA|en|/ˈpɒs.t͡ʃə.məs/|/ˈpɒs.t͡ʃʊ.məs/}}
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-posthumous.wav|Audio (Southern England)}}

===Adjective===
{{en-adj|-}}

# After the [[death]] of someone.
#* {{quote-book |en |title=[[:s:en:The Personal Memoirs of U. S. Grant|The Personal Memoirs of U.&nbsp;S. Grant]] |chapter=[[:s:en:Personal Memoirs of U. S. Grant/Chapter IV|Chapter&nbsp;IV]] |page=[[:s:en:Page:Personal Memoirs of Ulysses S. Grant Volume I.pdf/78|68]] |author={{w|Ulysses S. Grant}} |year=1885 |publisher={{w|Charles L. Webster & Co.}} |place=New York, United States |passage=The most favorable '''posthumous''' history the stay-at-home traitor can hope for is—oblivion.}}
# Taking place after one's own death.
#: {{ux|en|Artists obscure during their life often receive '''posthumous''' recognition, too late for them to enjoy.}}
# In reference to a [[work]], published after the [[author]]'s death.
#: {{ux|en|His memoirs were his '''posthumous''' revenge on enemies he dared not take on alive.}}
#* {{quote-web|en|url=http://www.theguardian.com/commentisfree/2012/apr/17/tupac-hologram-coachella-festival|title=Tupac's hologram reflects another milestone in his mythology |author=Alex Macpherson|date=2012-04-17|work=The Guardian|passage=Eight '''posthumous''' albums have been released to date – two more than the man managed in his lifetime – often with conspiracy-baiting titles such as Still I Rise and Tupac Resurrection.}}
# {{lb|en|originally}} Born after the death of one's father.
#: {{ux|en|'''Posthumous''' orphans never even knew their fathers.}}

